Object-pools.content:
In garbage-collected languages, object pools provide a way to eliminate runtime memory allocation and garbage collection for critical code paths. By preallocating a fixed set of reusable objects, an application can avoid creating new instances during execution, which would otherwise trigger garbage collection pauses or heap churn. Instead of allocating and discarding objects dynamically, code can acquire objects from the pool, reset their state, and return them for future reuse. This technique is particularly valuable in low-latency or real-time systems where predictable performance is essential and even small, transient allocations would introduce unacceptable jitter.
Every dynamic allocation increases allocation pressure on the heap, potentially triggering more frequent garbage collections and introducing latency spikes. Worse, garbage collectors may enforce safepoints—global pauses where all threads must synchronize—even for minor collections, impacting throughput and real-time responsiveness.
public class Pool&lt;T&gt; {
    private final T[] pool;
	private int size;

	@SuppressWarnings(&quot;unchecked&quot;)
    public Pool(Supplier&lt;T&gt; factory, int size) {
		this.pool = (T[]) new Object[size];  // arrays are reified at runtime
		this.size = size;
		for (int = 0; i &lt; size; i++) {
			pool[i] = factory.get();
		}
    }

	public T acquire() {
		T obj = pool[--size];
		pool[size] = null;
		return obj;
	}

	public void release(T obj) {
		pool[size++] = requireNonNull(obj);
	}
}
See Zero GC Java for more details.
